to will; to be pleased by.", "tags": ["ergative", "intransitive"], "examples": ["Just do as you please.", "He doesn't think, he just says whatever he pleases."], "quotes": [{"text": "Whatsoeuer the Lord pleased, that did he in heauen and in earth: in the Seas, and all deepe places.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Psalms 135:6:"}, {"text": "“Will any gentleman please to get outside and make room for a lady?”", "reference": "1870 July 16, “Our Idler’s Gossip”, in Bell’s Life in Sydney and Sporting Chronicle, volume XXVII, number 708, new series, [Sydney, N.S.W.], page [3], column 2:"}], "synonyms": ["desire", "will"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "haluta"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "mieliä"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "lystätä"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "belieben"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "szeretne"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "akar"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kíván"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kedve tartja"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "kér"}, {"language": {"code": "ko", "name": "Korean"}, "word": "바라다"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adverb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/pliːz/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/pliz/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "puh-lease", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Used to make a polite request.", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["Please, pass the bread.", "Would you please sign this form?", "Could you tell me the time, please?", "May I take your order, please?", "Q: Should I call him to confirm? A: Please do."], "quotes": [{"text": "(Michael): Yuri Andropov! What are you doing in my closet of anxieties again?\n(Yuri): Uh, oh. This is not 1600 Pennsylvania Ave., Washington, D.C.?\n(Michael): Does it look like it? You're in the wrong nightmare again!!\n(Yuri): ★@#*!?! Soviet maps ... worth nothing! Give, please, directions to White House.", "reference": "1983 July 10, Berkeley Breathed, Bloom County, spoken by Yuri Andropov:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "interjection", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/pliːz/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/pliz/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "puh-lease", "tags": ["alternative"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(often with yes) Used as an affirmative to an offer.", "tags": ["often"], "examples": ["Near-synonym: thank you", "May I help you? —(Yes,) please.", "D'you mind if I open the window? —Please do."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["thank you"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, "word": "asseblief"}, {"language": {"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, "word": "kanalla"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "мо́ля"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "kiitos"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "kyllä kiitos"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "mieluusti"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "oikein mielellään"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "bitte"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "gerne"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "hogyne"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "köszönettel veszem"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "köszönettel venném"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "(nagyon/igazán) megköszönném"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "prego"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "An expression of annoyance, impatience, or exasperation.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Near-synonyms: enough, enough already, that's enough, no more, cut it out, knock it off, shut up, STFU", "Please with that damn harmonica!", "Please with all the ads, I just want to watch the movie."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["enough", "enough already", "that's enough", "no more", "cut it out", "knock it off", "shut up", "STFU"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "interjection", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/pliːz/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/pliz/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [], "senses": [{"definition": "(Cincinnati) Said as a request to repeat information.", "tags": ["Cincinnati"], "examples": ["Customer while ordering: Can I get a [unintelligible]? Restaurant employee: Please?"], "quotes": [{"text": "Fellow: May I have a few days off to get married?\nReply, in the Cincinnati idiom by a boss who had heard the sound but not the sense:\nBoss: Please?", "reference": "August 1973, “Bitte or Bitter?”, in Cincinnati, page 109:"}, {"text": "Even though I heard it was supposed to be German-Catholic background, there’s only one thing German — they say ‘please’ [for the more common ‘pardon me’], which comes from bitte.", "reference": "September 1978, Virginia Watson-Rouslin, “A Foreign View”, in Cincinnati, page 110:"}, {"text": "[…] He explained in broken English that one of his daughters was ill and he probably could not be there. I did not understand all that he said, so asked, ‘Please?’ per Cincinnati custom. ‘There is no need to plead. I will be there if she is feeling better,’ he replied.", "reference": "September 1979, “Winners: Contest No. 13—The Laugh’s On Us”, in Cincinnati, volume 12, number 12, page 15:"}, {"text": "Cincinnati are some of the most polite persons I have ever met in the US. When asking someone a question, instead of saying “Excuse me,” or “Pardon,” they say “Please?”", "reference": "5 May 1998, Jose I. Sarasua, “Come to Cincinnati... Please?”, in Cost Engineering, volume 40, number 5, page 9:"}, {"text": "By the same token, one contestant who doesn’t hear a particular question could say “Pardon me?” while another could say “Please?” Again, neither would be lying if he said he was from Ohio.", "reference": "April 2001, Jeff Robinson, “Say what?”, in Ohio Magazine, archived from the original on 02 Apr 2019, page 77:"}, {"text": "In Maine, where as much as a quarter of the population has French ancestry, you may hear a stray hair called a couette, and in parts of Ohio please is used in the same way as the German bitte, to invite a person to repeat something just said — apparently a remnant of the bilingual schooling once available in Cincinnati.", "reference": "2008, Henry Hitchings, The Secret Life of Words: How English Became English, →ISBN, page 255:"}, {"text": "Ellen grew up outside of Cincinnati and believed her own talk was the “norm,” while others were speakers of dialects. She was in graduate school before she learned that not all people say, Please? to mean Can you repeat that?", "reference": "2011, Ellen McIntyre, Nancy Hulan, Vicky Layne, Reading Instruction for Diverse Classrooms: Research-Based, Culturally Responsive Practice, Guilford Press, →ISBN, page 72:"}], "synonyms": ["pardon", "pardon me", "beg pardon", "excuse me", "say again", "come again", "what's that", "what"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/please", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}